# Every `between` as filed, as a cross-document reference. One row per reference: which layer of
# which other document a composer says the double counting runs against.
#
# The second `pm:ForeignId` reference
#   The model has two `pm:ForeignId` references. An `asrt:Part` is named by
#   `composition/part_references.sqlc`; this names `asrt:Elimination/between`. Anyone enumerating
#   what this model points at in other documents from the parts alone gets half the answer. A
#   refusal reasoned from `elimination`, a magnitude BPMN genuinely does not need, is not about
#   `between`.
#
# Why no rule reaches it
#   The reason is structural, not an oversight. This tree's pressure is *a rule reads only what it
#   needs, so a field no rule reads is under no pressure to exist*. `between` is the one
#   cross-document reference that no rule may check: the schema licenses an unresolvable one
#   outright, because *a group eliminating against a member that files nothing, or against a layer
#   folded into a different composed layer, is ordinary*. So no rule can pull it into view.
#
# Its companion is an observation, not a check
#   `eliminations/resolved.sqlc` is the half that lands and this is the whole, and their difference
#   is reported rather than asserted, the way `coupling_search` reports. Writing
#   `checks/unresolved_part.sqlc`'s shape here would accuse the filing the schema names as ordinary.
#
# No foreign key
#   Exactly as `composition/part_references.sqlc` has none: a notation may name a document nobody
#   in this corpus filed, and that is a state and not an error.
-- asrt:Elimination/asrt:between, typed asrt:FiledLayer, whose filing is a pm:ForeignId.
SELECT b.composition, b.composed_layer, b.quantity, b.seq,
       b.party, b.notation, b.layer, b.regime
FROM (
    :compose(eliminations/between.sqlc)
) b
